== Weyl groups and Coxeter transformations <sec:weyl-coxeter>

In this section, we examine the geometry of a certain real vector space
associated with the graphs discussed in the last section.

#definition[
  Given a valued graph $T$ with no loops (edges from a vertex to itself), we
  form the real vector space $RR^T$ with $RR$-basis elements $v_x$ corresponding
  to the vertices $x$ in $T$, and with symmetric bilinear form given by the
  symmetrised Cartan matrix
  $ (v_x, v_y) = tilde(c)_(x y). $

  The *Weyl group*#idx("Weyl group")#idx("group", "Weyl") $W(T)$ is the group
  generated by the reflections#idx("reflection")
  $ w_x (v) = v - (2(v, v_x))/((v_x, v_x)) v_x. $

  It is easy to check that the $w_x$ are transformations of order two preserving
  the bilinear form, and fixing the hyperplane perpendicular to $x$. Note that
  if $Q$ is a (modulated) quiver with underlying valued graph $T$, and $V$ is a
  representation of $Q$, then the dimension of $V$ is in a natural way a vector
  in $RR^T$. Namely the coefficient of $v_x$ is the dimension over $Delta_x$ of
  $V_x$. This vector is called the *dimension vector*#idx(
    "dimension",
    "vector",
  )#idx("vector", "dimension") of $V$.
] <def:weyl-group>

#lemma[
  For $x != y$, the product $w_x w_y$ has order $2$, $3$, $4$, $6$ or $infinity$
  according as $c_(x y) c_(y x)$ is $0$, $1$, $2$, $3$ or at least $4$
  respectively.
] <lem:reflection-product-order>

#proof[
  #source(133)This is an easy exercise in two dimensional geometry.
]

#proposition[
  Let $T$ be a finite connected valued graph without loops.
  + $T$ is a finite Dynkin diagram if and only if the bilinear form $(med, med)$
    is positive definite on $RR^T$.
  + $T$ is a Euclidean diagram if and only if $(med, med)$ is positive
    semidefinite and not definite on $RR^T$. In this case every null vector is a
    multiple of the vector given by the additive function shown in
    Definition~@def:dynkin-euclidean.
  + If $T$ is neither Dynkin nor Euclidean, then there is a vector $v in RR^T$
    with non-negative integral coordinates with respect to the basis vectors
    $v_x$, such that $(v, v) < 0$.
] <prop:bilinear-form-definite>

#proof[
  Suppose $T$ is a Euclidean diagram. By Lemma~@lem:euclidean-additive[(i)],
  there is an additive function $x |-> n_x$ on $T$. For a particular value of
  $x$, we have $sum_(y != x) n_y c_(y x) slash n_x = -2$, and so the norm of a
  typical vector $sum_x a_x v_x$ is
  $
    #aligned-rows(
      (
        $(sum_x a_x v_x, sum_x a_x v_x)$,
        $= 2 sum_x a_x^2 f_x + sum_(x != y) a_x a_y c_(x y) f_y$,
      ),
      (
        $$,
        $= -1/2 sum_(x != y) ((a_x^2 n_y c_(y x) f_x)/n_x
          + (a_y^2 n_x c_(x y) f_y)/n_y)
        + sum_(x != y) a_x a_y c_(x y) f_y$,
      ),
      (
        $$,
        $= -1/2 sum_(x != y) n_x n_y c_(x y) f_y (a_x slash n_x
          - a_y slash n_y)^2.$,
      ),
    )
  $
  which is positive semidefinite since the $c_(x y)$ are negative for $x != y$,
  and the $n_x$ and $f_x$ are positive. Moreover for a null vector, all the
  $a_x slash n_x$ must have the same value, so that the null space is one
  dimensional.

  Since every Dynkin diagram is strictly smaller than a Euclidean diagram, it
  follows that $(med, med)$ is positive definite on the Dynkin diagrams.

  If $T$ is neither Euclidean nor Dynkin then by Lemma~@lem:dynkin-or-euclidean
  there is a Euclidean diagram $T'$ which is strictly smaller than $T$. If $T'$
  contains all the points of $T$, then a null vector for $T'$ has negative norm
  for $T$. Otherwise choose a point of $T$ adjacent to a point of $T'$, and add
  a small enough positive rational multiple of the corresponding basis element
  to the null vector for $T'$, to obtain a vector of negative norm. A suitable
  multiple of this vector has non-negative integral coordinates with respect to
  the basis vectors $v_x$.
]

#proposition[
  + Suppose $T$ is a finite Dynkin diagram. Then $W(T)$ is a finite group of
    automorphisms of $RR^T$. There is no non-zero vector in $RR^T$ fixed by the
    whole of $W(T)$.
  + Suppose $T$ is a Euclidean diagram. Let $bold(n)$ be the null vector given
    by the additive function $x |-> n_x$ shown in
    Definition~@def:dynkin-euclidean. Then $W(T)$ preserves
    $chevron.l bold(n) chevron.r$ and acts as a finite group of automorphisms of
    $RR^T slash chevron.l bold(n) chevron.r$.
] <prop:weyl-group-finite>

#proof[
  + Since the matrices in $W(T)$ have integer entries with respect to our basis
    $v_x$, $W(T)$ is a discrete subgroup of the compact orthogonal group. Thus
    it is finite. A vector in $RR^T$ fixed by the whole of $W(T)$ would have to
    #source(134)have zero inner product with each basis vector $v_x$, and hence
    it would have to be zero.
  + Since $chevron.l bold(n) chevron.r$ is the radical of $(med, med)$,
    $chevron.l bold(n) chevron.r$ is preserved by $W(T)$. Now $W(T)$ acts as a
    discrete subgroup of the compact orthogonal group on
    $RR^T slash chevron.l bold(n) chevron.r$, and this action is therefore
    finite.
]

#definition[
  A *Coxeter transformation*#idx("Coxeter", "transformation")#idx(
    "transformation",
    "Coxeter",
  ) on $RR^T$ is a linear transformation obtained by applying all the $w_x$ once
  each, in some order.

  If $T$ is a finite Dynkin or Euclidean diagram and $c$ is a Coxeter
  transformation, then by the above Proposition, $c$ has finite order $h$ on
  $RR^T$ in the first case, and on $RR^T slash chevron.l bold(n) chevron.r$ in
  the second. This number $h$ is called the *Coxeter number*#idx(
    "Coxeter",
    "number",
  )#idx("number, Coxeter").

  If $T$ is Euclidean and $v in RR^T$ then the *defect*#idx(
    "defect",
    "of a vector",
  )#idx("vector", "defect of") $partial_c (v)$ of $v$ with respect to a Coxeter
  transformation $c$ is defined by
  $ c^h (v) = v + partial_c (v) bold(n). $
  Thus $partial_c$ is a linear form $RR^T -> RR$, and
  $chevron.l bold(n) chevron.r subset.eq Ker(partial_c)$.
] <def:coxeter-transformation>

#exercise[
  Show that if $T$ is a tree then all the Coxeter transformations are conjugate
  in $W(T)$.
]

#lemma[
  Suppose $T$ is a Euclidean diagram. The following two conditions on a vector
  $v in RR^T$ are equivalent:
  + $v$ has infinitely many images under $c$.
  + $partial_c (v) != 0$.

  If #statement-comment[(i)] and #statement-comment[(ii)] are satisfied then
  some image of $v$ under some power of $c$ has negative coordinates.
] <lem:defect>

#proof[
  This is clear from the previous discussion.
]

#definition[
  If $T$ is a finite Dynkin diagram, then the *root system*#idx(
    "root system",
  )#idx("system", "root") associated to $T$ is the finite subset $Phi$ of $RR^T$
  consisting of the images under $W(T)$ of the basis vectors $v_x$. These are
  exactly the root systems in the sense of for example Humphreys
  [@bib:Humphreys1972, Chapter~III]. The elements of $Phi$ are called the
  *roots*. Since the matrices in $W(T)$ have integer entries, all roots are
  integral combinations of the basis vectors. Reflection in the hyperplane
  perpendicular to a root permutes the set of roots. If $v$ is a root we write
  $w_v$ for this reflection.

  A non-zero vector which is a non-negative linear combination of the $v_x$ is
  called *positive*#idx("positive vector")#idx("vector", "positive"), and a
  vector $v$ is *negative*#idx("negative vector")#idx("vector", "negative") if
  $-v$ is positive.
] <def:root-system>

#lemma[
  Suppose $T$ is a Dynkin diagram.
  + If $v$ is a root then so is $-v$.
  + Suppose $v$ and $v'$ are roots, neither a multiple of the other, and suppose
    $(v, v') > 0$. Then either $(v', v') = 2(v, v')$ or $(v, v) = 2(v, v')$. The
    vector $v - v'$ is also a root.
  + If $v$ is a positive root and $w_x$ is a reflection then either $w_x (v)$ is
    positive or $v = v_x$ and $w_x (v) = -v_x$.
  + #source(135)Every root is either positive or negative.
] <lem:roots>

#proof[
  + $w_v (v) = -v$.
  + The reflection perpendicular to $v$ of $v'$ is a root, as is the reflection
    of $v$ perpendicular to $v'$, and $2(v, v') slash (v, v)$ and
    $2(v, v') slash (v', v')$ are integers. If $theta$ is the angle between $v$
    and $v'$, then
    $
      scaled(((2(v, v'))/((v, v)))) scaled(((2(v, v'))/((v', v'))))
      = (4(v, v')^2)/((v, v)(v', v')) = 4 cos^2 theta < 4.
    $
    Thus if $(v, v') > 0$ then either $(v', v') = 2(v, v')$ or
    $(v, v) = 2(v, v')$. In the former case $w_(v') (v) = v - v'$, while in the
    latter case $w_v (v') = v' - v$ and we use (i).
  + Suppose $v$ is an image $w(v_y)$ under $W(T)$ of the basis vector $v_y$. If
    $v != v_x$ and $w_x (v)$ is not positive, then $(v, v_x) > 0$. As in (ii),
    we have two cases according as $2(v, v_x) = (v_x, v_x)$ or
    $2(v, v_x) = (v, v) = (v_y, v_y)$. In the former case, $w_x (v) = v - v_x$.
    Since $v$ must involve a strictly positive multiple of $v_x$ in order that
    $(v, v_x) > 0$, we conclude that $w_x (v)$ is again positive.

    In the latter case, we argue as follows. There is an isomorphism
    $rho : RR^T -> RR^(T^op)$ given by setting
    $rho(sum_x a_x v_x) = sum_x a_x f_x v_x$, and it is easy to check that this
    isomorphism commutes with the actions of the reflections $w_x$, so that the
    two Weyl groups are isomorphic. In fact the rôle of $rho$ is to make long
    roots short and vice versa. We now look at the vector
    $v' = w(v_y) in RR^(T^op)$. If $2(v, v_x) = (v, v)$ in $RR^T$ then
    $2(v', v_x) = (v_x, v_x)$ in $RR^(T^op)$ and so we may apply the argument of
    the previous paragraph to deduce that $w_x (v')$ is positive, and hence
    $w_x (v)$ is positive.
  + This easily follows from (iii).
]

#lemma[
  Suppose $T$ is a Dynkin diagram, with vertices $x_1, dots, x_n$ and
  corresponding basis elements $v_1, dots, v_n$ of $RR^T$, reflections
  $w_1, dots, w_n$ on $RR^T$ and Coxeter transformation $c = w_1 dots w_n$.
  + The transformation $c$ has no non-zero fixed points on $RR^T$.
  + Given any vector $v in RR^T$, for some value of $m gt.eq 0$ the vector
    $c^m (v)$ is not positive.
] <lem:coxeter-no-fixed-points>

#proof[
  + Suppose $v = sum_i r_i v_i$ and $c(v) = v$. Then
    $ w_1 (v) = w_2 dots w_n (v) $
    and so the multiple of $v_1$ in $w_1 (v)$ is still $r_1$, and hence
    $w_1 (v) = v$. Repeating this argument, we see that
    $ w_2 (v) = w_3 dots w_n (v) $
    and hence $w_2 (v) = v$. Continuing this way, we see that $v$ is fixed by
    all the $w_i$, and is hence zero.
  + If $c^m (v)$ is positive for all $m$, then $sum_(i=0)^(h-1) c^i (v)$ is a
    positive vector fixed by $c$, contradicting (i).
]
